  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... Satellite and KMLB radar imagery show isolated to \nscattered showers and storms developing along and ahead of the east \ncoast sea breeze this afternoon. High-Res guidance has generally \nshown the greatest coverage building across the interior from \nOsceola northward (~50-60%) late this afternoon and into the \nevening. Despite high PWATs analyzed around 2.0\", the 15Z XMR \nsounding and updated MCO ACARS profiles show a layer of dry air \nabove 850 mb. While shear remains weak, isolated strong updrafts \nwhich interact with the layer of dry air aloft will be capable of \nthunderstorm wind gusts of 35-50 mph and frequent lightning \nstrikes. Outside of showers and storms, hot conditions continue, \nand temperatures will continue to climb through the 90s. A Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect through 8PM for peak heat index values \nexceeding 108 degrees. Shower and storm activity diminishes or \nmoves west of the local forecast area by 9PM with dry conditions \novernight. Low temperatures fall to range the mid to upper 70s. \n\nWeekend... A 500mb ridge continues to build over the southern U.S. \nthis weekend while a surface ridge axis remains extended across \ncentral Florida. A thin ribbon of higher moisture lifts from the \nsoutheast on Saturday providing the best chances for scattered \nshowers and storms across portions of Okeechobee and the interior \nTreasure Coast (30-50%), and widely isolated coverage exists \nfurther north (20% or less). The surge of locally higher moisture \nwill be short lived, followed by a plume of Saharan dry air. This \nshould keep below seasonal rain chances on Sunday with a 20% \nchance of isolated showers and storms across the area. Despite low\nrain chances, efficient surface heating should support a few \nquick updrafts, and tall storms which interact with dry air aloft \nwill be capable of localized downburst wind gusts of 35-50 mph.\n\nWith little relief from showers and storms for most of the area, \nexpect a period of impactful heat to continue. High temperatures \nclimb into the mid to upper 90s each day, and a few daily records \nmay be within reach across the north interior. Peak heat index \nvalues are forecast to climb near or above 108F, and Heat Advisories \nlook likely to continue into the weekend. Be sure to practice heat \nsafety, and never leave children alone in the car. Look before \nyou lock!\n\nMonday-Thursday... The structure of the 500mb high broadens over the \nsouthern U.S. into next week but should still hold influence over \neast central Florida. Surface low pressure is forecast to move \noffshore the mid-Atlantic and northeastern seaboard on Monday, \nand an associated frontal boundary sags into the southeast U.S. \nThe front remains north of central Florida, although it should \nstill increase moisture locally. Low rain chances (20-30%) remain \nfocused near and north of Orlando and Cape Canaveral on Monday \nbefore a more seasonal pattern of scattered showers and storms \nreturns areawide into mid week. A period of impactful heat \ncontinues with above normal temperatures in the mid to upper 90s \nforecast each day.",
